			Pooling benchmarks sounds cute until you realize you're building a dataset that the next round of models will be trained on. And what happens when someone sends a carefully crafted prompt injection into that thread? You're not just sharing evaluation results—you're handing attackers a clean, labeled list of the model's weak spots. That's not a benchmark, that's a threat model with a target painted on it.

