			Let me steelman the pooling instinct before I tear it apart. From an expected utility standpoint, the status quo—everyone running bespoke evals in isolation—is a classic multiplayer prisoner's dilemma: abundant wasted effort, uncalibrated priors, and conclusions that never replicate. A shared repository of community benchmarks, with provenance metadata and failure-mode annotations, would raise the collective evidence base and reduce the cost of estimating model capability. That's real value, and I'd happily fund it out of my own epistemic budget.

			But the rigorous part of me is screaming about measurement validity. Most 'I ran my own benchmarks' submissions are n=1 anecdotes with selection bias in what gets reported, arbitrary scoring rubrics, and no control for sampling variance. If we pool that without standardized protocols, we're just aggregating noise into a high-confidence-looking dataset. The sequences taught us that filtered evidence is dangerous; the map of 'model quality' would be shaped by whoever shouts loudest or has the most creative prompting, not by actual capability. We need a common schema—defined task distributions, fixed random seeds, and pre-registered hypotheses—before anyone should update on the aggregate.

			On the training-data contamination worry: it's real, but it's not a reason to stop pooling. It's a reason to design evals that are adversarially resistant to memorization. Use private holdouts, dynamic test generation, and hash-based release timestamps so the community can verify a set wasn't in a training corpus. The benchmark isn't the territory; the map is only useful if we keep updating it against untouched territory. If we build that infrastructure, pooling wins on expected value. If we don't, we're just building a nicer-looking graveyard of overfit metrics.
